---
import CaseLayout from '../../layouts/CaseLayout.astro';
import CaseSection from '../../components/CaseSection.astro';

const next = {
  href: '/work/point-clouds/',
  category: '3D geometry',
  title: 'Point-cloud alignment',
  image: '/images/pcd-card.webp',
  alt: 'Aligned point cloud inside a custom viewer with a raw misaligned inset',
};

const classes: Array<[string, number]> = [
  ['Marrow', 0.927],
  ['dead_knot', 0.784],
  ['live_knot', 0.693],
  ['resin', 0.680],
  ['knot_missing', 0.648],
  ['crack', 0.553],
  ['knot_with_crack', 0.417],
  ['quartzity', 0.308],
];
---

<CaseLayout
  title="Wood surface defect detection"
  description="An end-to-end YOLO pipeline for wood surface defects: dataset preparation, training, validation, per-class failure analysis, and live video inference."
  index="02"
  category="Applied computer vision"
  subtitle="A full inspection pipeline for a natural material: eight defect classes, a long-tailed dataset, and the honest version of what the detector gets right."
  meta={[
    { label: 'Role', value: 'Pipeline design · training · evaluation' },
    { label: 'Timeline', value: '2025' },
    { label: 'Type', value: 'Applied CV / manufacturing' },
    { label: 'Stack', value: 'Ultralytics YOLO · OpenCV · yt-dlp · Kaggle' },
  ]}
  next={next}
>
  <Fragment slot="media">
    <nav class="case-toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <a href="#problem">01 / Problem</a>
      <a href="#pipeline">02 / Pipeline</a>
      <a href="#results">03 / Results</a>
      <a href="#failures">04 / Failure analysis</a>
      <a href="#next">05 / What I'd change</a>
    </nav>

    <figure class="case-lead-media">
      <div class="figure__media">
        <img
          src="/images/wood-card.webp"
          alt="Wood defect detection contact sheet: a plank with live-knot and dead-knot boxes, plus four validation crops"
          width="1200"
          height="900"
          fetchpriority="high"
          decoding="async"
        />
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        Inference and validation views from the finished pipeline. The card is a composite of real predictions, not a mock-up.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </Fragment>

  <CaseSection number="01" title="Wood is a natural material. Its defects are not neatly separable." id="problem">
    <p class="case-lead">
      A crack, a resin pocket, and a dead knot can occupy the same few pixels. The classes are visually close, the
      dataset is long-tailed, and the cost of a miss is a bad plank that ships.
    </p>
    <p>
      The task was to build an inspection pipeline, not just train a model: acquire the data, convert it to YOLO
      format, split it without leakage, train and resume runs, validate with per-class metrics, run inference on
      images, and finally run the detector on a live video stream.
    </p>
    <p>
      The public dataset — Kaggle's <strong>Large-Scale Image Dataset of Wood Surface Defects</strong> — defines eight
      classes: <strong>quartzity, live_knot, Marrow, resin, dead_knot, knot_with_crack, knot_missing, crack</strong>.
      Some are self-explanatory. Some are not, and that ambiguity shows up in the results.
    </p>
    <div class="case-callout">
      A detector is only useful if the evaluation survives contact with the class distribution. The case study keeps
      the weak classes in the frame instead of reporting a single aggregate number.
    </div>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection number="02" title="One pipeline, from raw labels to a live stream." id="pipeline">
    <p class="case-lead">
      The repository is structured as a repeatable path. Each stage can run on its own, which matters when training is
      interrupted and a run has to resume from a checkpoint.
    </p>

    <div class="case-pipeline" aria-label="Wood defect pipeline">
      <div class="pipeline-step">
        <span class="pipeline-step__index">01</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Acquire</h3>
          <p>Download the Kaggle dataset, unpack it, and preserve the original class folders and annotations.</p>
          <span class="pipeline-step__meta">Kaggle API · Python</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pipeline-step">
        <span class="pipeline-step__index">02</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Prepare</h3>
          <p>Convert annotations into YOLO label files, inspect class frequencies, and create train / validation / test splits.</p>
          <span class="pipeline-step__meta">YOLO format · stratified split</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pipeline-step">
        <span class="pipeline-step__index">03</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Train &amp; resume</h3>
          <p>Fine-tune a pretrained detector at 320&nbsp;px, checkpoint to Google Drive, and resume the run from the last checkpoint when Colab disconnects.</p>
          <span class="pipeline-step__meta">Ultralytics YOLO · 200 epochs · AMP</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pipeline-step">
        <span class="pipeline-step__index">04</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Validate</h3>
          <p>Report precision, recall, mAP50, mAP50-95, per-class AP, and a normalized confusion matrix.</p>
          <span class="pipeline-step__meta">best.pt · validation split</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="pipeline-step">
        <span class="pipeline-step__index">05</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Infer</h3>
          <p>Run batched image inference and a live path that resolves a YouTube stream with yt-dlp and decodes it with OpenCV.</p>
          <span class="pipeline-step__meta">OpenCV · yt-dlp · annotated frames</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="case-code">
      <div class="case-code__bar"><span>train.py / resume from checkpoint</span><span>Python</span></div>
      <pre><code><span class="tok-com"># resume the same run instead of starting from scratch</span>
<span class="tok-key">model</span> = YOLO(<span class="tok-str">"last.pt"</span>)
model.train(
    data=<span class="tok-str">"./dataset/data.yaml"</span>,
    epochs=<span class="tok-str">200</span>,
    imgsz=<span class="tok-str">320</span>,
    project=<span class="tok-str">"wood_defects"</span>,
    name=<span class="tok-str">"run_results"</span>,
    resume=<span class="tok-key">True</span>,
)

<span class="tok-com"># live stream: yt-dlp resolves the URL, OpenCV decodes frames</span>
<span class="tok-key">stream</span> = yt_dlp.YoutubeDL({<span class="tok-str">"format"</span>: <span class="tok-str">"best[ext=mp4]"</span>}).extract_info(url, download=<span class="tok-key">False</span>)
cap = cv2.VideoCapture(stream[<span class="tok-str">"url"</span>])
<span class="tok-key">while</span> cap.isOpened():
    ok, frame = cap.read()
    <span class="tok-key">if not</span> ok:
        <span class="tok-key">break</span>
    frame = model.predict(frame, conf=<span class="tok-str">0.4</span>)[<span class="tok-str">0</span>].plot()
    cv2.imshow(<span class="tok-str">"wood_defects"</span>, frame)</code></pre>
    </div>
    <p class="case-note">
      Simplified for readability. The repository keeps the run configuration, dataset paths, and stream handling in
      separate cells so each stage can be rerun independently.
    </p>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection number="03" title="The best checkpoint is not the last checkpoint." id="results">
    <p class="case-lead">
      The run completed 200 epochs. The best validation score arrived at epoch 109 — and the final epoch was worse.
      Reporting both is the difference between a benchmark and a useful checkpoint.
    </p>

    <div class="case-metrics">
      <div class="case-metric">
        <strong>0.626</strong>
        <span>mAP50 at epoch 109 · best checkpoint</span>
      </div>
      <div class="case-metric">
        <strong>0.324</strong>
        <span>mAP50-95 at epoch 109</span>
      </div>
      <div class="case-metric">
        <strong>8</strong>
        <span>defect classes · 200 epochs · 320 px</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>
      At the best checkpoint, precision was <strong>0.670</strong> and recall <strong>0.621</strong>. At the final epoch,
      mAP50 fell to <strong>0.568</strong> and mAP50-95 to <strong>0.289</strong>. The pipeline therefore selects
      <strong>best.pt</strong> for inference, and the checkpoint is treated as part of the artifact — not an
      afterthought.
    </p>

    <div class="case-pair">
      <figure class="case-figure case-figure--light">
        <div class="figure__media">
          <img src="/images/wood-pr-curve.webp" alt="Precision-recall curves for all eight wood defect classes, with an all-class mAP50 of 0.626" width="1400" height="933" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </div>
        <figcaption>Precision-recall curves at the best checkpoint. Marrow is easy; quartzity and knot_with_crack are not.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="case-figure case-figure--light">
        <div class="figure__media">
          <img src="/images/wood-confusion.webp" alt="Normalized confusion matrix for the eight wood defect classes" width="1400" height="1050" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </div>
        <figcaption>Normalized confusion matrix. The off-diagonal mass is where visually similar defects collide.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <figure class="case-figure case-figure--light">
      <div class="figure__media">
        <img src="/images/wood-val-pred.webp" alt="Validation batch predictions for wood surface defects, with boxes and confidence scores" width="1408" height="640" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
      </div>
      <figcaption>Validation predictions from the best checkpoint. Low-confidence boxes are left in the frame so the failure modes stay visible.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection number="04" title="Where the detector is weak — and why." id="failures">
    <p class="case-lead">
      The aggregate mAP hides a two-tier problem. Four classes are usable, two are marginal, and two are effectively
      research targets.
    </p>

    <table class="case-table">
      <caption>Per-class AP at IoU 0.5 · best checkpoint</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Class</th><th scope="col">AP</th><th scope="col">Relative performance</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {classes.map(([name, ap]) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{name}</td>
            <td>{ap.toFixed(3)}</td>
            <td><div class="ap-track"><span style={`width: ${Math.round(ap * 100)}%`}></span></div></td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      <strong>Marrow</strong> reaches 0.927 AP, and <strong>dead_knot</strong> and <strong>live_knot</strong> are
      respectable. <strong>Quartzity</strong> (0.308) and <strong>knot_with_crack</strong> (0.417) are the weak points.
      The confusion matrix suggests why: quartzity is confused with background, and knot_with_crack shares visual
      structure with both cracks and live knots.
    </p>
    <p>
      Part of the cause is resolution. Training at <strong>320&nbsp;px</strong> is fast, but a hairline crack can be only a
      few pixels wide after letterboxing. Part of it is label ambiguity: the boundary between a crack and a
      knot_with_crack is not always obvious, even to a human annotator. A single aggregate metric would have hidden
      both problems.
    </p>

    <div class="case-callout">
      The useful question is not “what is the mAP?” It is “which class fails, on what kind of image, and what would it
      cost to fix it?” The per-class table is the start of that conversation.
    </div>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection number="05" title="What I would change next." id="next">
    <p class="case-lead">
      The pipeline is a solid baseline, not a finished inspection product. The next iteration would attack resolution,
      class balance, and deployment together.
    </p>

    <ul class="case-list">
      <li><strong>Raise the input resolution.</strong> Train at 640&nbsp;px or use tiled inference for thin cracks, then measure the latency cost honestly.</li>
      <li><strong>Rebalance the long tail.</strong> Targeted collection for quartzity and knot_with_crack, class-aware sampling, and harder copy-paste augmentation.</li>
      <li><strong>Audit the labels.</strong> A second annotator on the confusable classes would tell us whether the model is wrong or the ground truth is.</li>
      <li><strong>Evaluate on a fixed camera.</strong> The public dataset is a proxy. A production inspection cell has fixed lighting, distance, and motion blur — and those should be in the test set.</li>
      <li><strong>Export and measure.</strong> ONNX or TensorRT, then report frames per second on the target hardware, not just on a Colab GPU.</li>
    </ul>

